Why the plugins are vendored rather than installed from the panel
----------------------------------------------------------------

TTT installs a plugin with `git clone` into the config directory. That
directory is not a mounted volume, so the clone would happen again in every new
VM, at first run, over the network. Vendoring puts the files in the image
instead, and pins them to a commit rather than to a moving branch.

Why dev-tools/ttt-plugins.json exists
-------------------------------------

TTT asks for approval before it loads a plugin whose name is absent from
~/.config/ttt/plugins.ttt.json, or whose manifest requests a permission that
the entry there does not grant. That file is a plain JSON array, so it can be
written ahead of time. Each entry here grants exactly the permissions in the
matching plugin.ttt.json, and no more. Neither plugin asks for system.exec,
network.http or network.rpc:

    markdown-preview  panel.editor, commands, editor.read
    omarchy-theme     fs.read  ["~/.local/state/omarchy"]
                      fs.write ["{config}/themes"]
                      settings, settings_keys ["theme"], commands

markdown-preview reads the open buffer and renders it. It cannot write a file,
run a command, or open a connection. omarchy-theme reads one directory and
writes one directory, both named above. "grantsVersion": 2 matches the constant
`localAccessGrants` in internal/plugin/registry.go. A lower value would make
TTT clear the entry's network.http grant on load. Neither plugin uses that
grant, so the value only keeps this file identical to what TTT would write.

Why the theme palette is NOT vendored
-------------------------------------

omarchy-theme reads ~/.local/state/omarchy/current/theme/colors.toml, and the
sibling vscode-theme.json when it exists. Those two files come from
bjarneo/omarchy-fireside-theme, which declares no licence, so its palette is
all rights reserved by default. This repository is public. Committing the
palette would redistribute it.

Instead sandbox.yaml mounts that directory into the VM read-only:

    ${SANDBOX_OMARCHY_THEME}:/home/agent/.local/state/omarchy/current/theme:ro

Nothing is copied into the repository, and the VM then follows whichever
Omarchy theme is active on the host. The plugin polls every two seconds, so a
theme switch on the host recolours the editor without a restart. Every file in
that directory is a colour definition. It was scanned for credentials before
this mount was added, and it holds none.

A note on diagrams
------------------

markdown-preview renders text only. TTT's whole image support is the setting
`image.protocol`, and internal/config/settings.go allows it three values: auto,
kitty and none. There is no sixel and no iterm2. The host terminal here is foot,
which supports sixel and not the Kitty graphics protocol. So no image can appear
in this editor, and a fenced mermaid block renders as highlighted source. See
docs/research/vm-editor-ttt.md.
